# URLs lychee must not fail the build on, each with the reason it is here.
# Delete an entry the moment its reason expires — an ignore list nobody prunes
# is how a genuinely broken link hides.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Release artefacts, which do not exist until the first release is cut.
#
# A blanket `https://github.com/nebula-token/.*` used to sit here, because the
# repository was private and every one of its URLs returned 404 to an anonymous
# checker, which is what lychee is. The repository went public on 2026-07-29, so
# the blanket is gone and every repository, blob and tree URL in the docs is
# checked for real from the next run — nebula-token/nebula-token is the canonical
# home and the ONLY repository, so those are the most load-bearing links here and
# they should not be exempt from anything.
#
# Both entries that once sat here have been retired, as this block said they
# must be. 1.0.1 shipped, so the skills archive and every `releases/tag/v*` URL
# resolve and are now checked for real. The `## [1.0.0]` heading links to its
# commit rather than to a release, because the v1.0.0 tag was withdrawn during
# the 1.0.1 transition — a commit URL is the honest target and needs no
# exemption.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Registry pages. The nine pre-emptive exemptions that sat here are gone: all
# ten registries carry 1.0.1, so every registry URL is reachable and is checked
# for real. Re-adding one would hide exactly the failure this project already
# had — Maven Central and the Go proxy silently carrying nothing.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Hosts that refuse automated requests.
#
# Not broken — deliberately hostile to link checkers. A 403 from these says
# nothing about whether the page exists.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
https://www.linkedin.com/.*
https://twitter.com/.*
https://x.com/.*

# npmjs.com package pages, ALL of them, this project's own included.
#
# Observed, not assumed: the `links` job's first real run failed on exactly one
# URL —
#   [403] https://www.npmjs.com/package/skills — Rejected status code: 403
# in an otherwise clean pass. The 403 is the registry's answer to an unattended
# client, not a fact about the `skills` package, so the pattern is the host.
#
# This is the one entry a release does NOT retire, which is why it sits here with
# the other hostile hosts instead of in the registry block above. That block says
# its URLs "become real at 1.0.0 and are then checked like anything else", which
# is true of the nine it still lists; npm is the exception, because
# https://www.npmjs.com/package/nebula-token will exist after 1.0.0 and will go
# on answering 403 to lychee. Deleting this on release day would turn a perfectly
# healthy link red.
https://www\.npmjs\.com/package/.*

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Example and placeholder hosts used in documentation. Reserved by RFC 2606 and
# RFC 6761 precisely so that they never resolve.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
https?://example\.(com|org|net).*
https?://localhost.*
https?://127\.0\.0\.1.*
postgres://.*
mysql://.*
